+ out='lll doc - work with docs (wiki pages, findings, decisions, PRDs)

Usage:
  lll doc new -s "slug" -t "title" [-k kind] [-b "body"]
                                                  create a doc; -b - reads the body
                                                  from stdin
  lll doc list                                    list docs (slug, kind, title), one
                                                  line each
  lll doc view SLUG [--raw] [--json]              show one doc with its linked issues;
                                                  --raw prints only the body;
                                                  --json prints the raw record
  lll doc edit SLUG [-b "body"] [-t "title"] [-k kind]
                                                  replace fields (at least one flag);
                                                  -b - reads the whole body from stdin
  lll doc delete SLUG [--force]                   delete; asks [y/N] unless --force
Flags:
  -s       URL-safe name for the doc, e.g. port-notes
  -t       human-readable title
  -k       wiki (default), finding, decision, or prd
  -b       body text ('\''-b -'\'' reads it from stdin)
  -a       workspace area the doc is about — conventionally a label'\''s name,
           so issues carrying that label surface the doc
  -p       comma-separated file/directory paths the doc is about, e.g.
           src/pb, pb/pb_migrations ('\''lll finding near'\'' matches by them)
  --raw    print only the body, no chrome
  --json   raw PocketBase JSON (linked issues expanded), pipe-friendly
  --force  skip the delete confirmation

Every verb works inside ONE team — the one LLL_TEAM or .lll.toml names. A
slug is unique per team, so two projects sharing a server both get an
'\''index'\''; read the other one with LLL_TEAM=THEIRS lll doc view index.

Docs link to issues with '\''lll issue link KEY-123 SLUG'\'' and unlink with
'\''lll issue unlink KEY-123 SLUG'\'' (see '\''lll issue --help'\''). A link stays
inside one team: the doc and the issue must belong to the same one.

Findings surface by area and path, not by search: '\''lll finding near <path>'\''
lists what is filed about a file or directory, '\''lll finding list [--area X]'\''
